      "lineNbr": 378,
      "author": {
        "id": 1040868
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-27T08:33:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "does rust have conventions around context manager naming or usage ?  or does it have conventions with methods providing a context manager on the fly such that it could be a method on Minijail ?\n\nor maybe something more heretical: since rust manages life cycles all the time, is there a situation where we would ever actively be leaking a jailed process ?  like, what if this was just how Minijail worked ?",
      "parentUuid": "f5012b30_7eff9a8f",
      "revId": "8f3e04cca1c2a3330e27b0b3efc542f8015a736a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "832bde43_67813d6a",
        "filename": "rust/minijail/src/lib.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 378,
      "author": {
        "id": 1299283
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-27T16:15:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e does rust have conventions around context manager naming or usage ?  or does it have conventions with methods providing a context manager on the fly such that it could be a method on Minijail ?\n\nRust\u0027s std::process::Command is probably the closest parallel I can think of and it has no builtin KillOnDrop functionality. In fact we implement our own KillOnDrop in sys_util here:\nhttps://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromiumos/platform/crosvm/+/HEAD:common/sys_util/src/signal.rs;l\u003d646\n\n\u003e or maybe something more heretical: since rust manages life cycles all the time, is there a situation where we would ever actively be leaking a jailed process ?  like, what if this was just how Minijail worked ?\n\nHeretical indeed ;). The lifecycle of the jail isn\u0027t always tied to the parent process. We could allow a way of leaking the jailed process intentionally. This would definitely be a deviation from how minijail is handled in C/C++, but that is OK IMO. There would be a good deal of refactoring needed, and drop isn\u0027t guaranteed to be called in the process dies ungracefully.",
      "parentUuid": "0c306e23_a350ed01",
      "revId": "8f3e04cca1c2a3330e27b0b3efc542f8015a736a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ea852ff1_10698430",
        "filename": "rust/minijail/src/lib.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 378,
      "author": {
        "id": 1076784
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-27T20:59:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "minijail is a library that is used in many places, so I do not think we can simply alter the established behavior.",
      "parentUuid": "832bde43_67813d6a",
      "revId": "8f3e04cca1c2a3330e27b0b3efc542f8015a736a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0b887d63_e91c88a7",
        "filename": "rust/minijail/src/lib.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 378,
      "author": {
        "id": 1040868
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-27T21:27:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "we\u0027re talking about Rust here.  minijail isn\u0027t an installed shared library.  the entire point is that Rust doesn\u0027t do stable ABI.\n\niiuc, Rust also follows semver, so communicating such changes would be by bumping the version number up.\n\nisn\u0027t this par for the course in the Rust world ?",
      "parentUuid": "ea852ff1_10698430",
      "revId": "8f3e04cca1c2a3330e27b0b3efc542f8015a736a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "aaad7a0f_e7a043e8",
        "filename": "rust/minijail/src/lib.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
